meta {
  name: A clash names every rule it broke
  type: http
  seq: 57
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/timetables/{{timetableId}}/slots
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "section_id": "{{sectionId}}",
    "period_id": "{{periodOneId}}",
    "day_of_week": 1,
    "label": "Supervised study",
    "teacher_id": "{{teacherId}}"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 409
  res.body.code: eq timetable_clash
  res.body.count: eq 2
  res.body.clashes[0].rule: eq student_set
  res.body.clashes[1].rule: eq teacher
}

docs {
  409 with code timetable_clash and both parties named: the existing slot
  under "left", the rejected proposal under "right", with the rule and the
  scope that caught it. scope is "pattern" when two weekly slots collide and
  "date" when a deviation puts them on the same day.

  TWO clashes come back, not one. Every rule the proposal breaks is reported
  separately against the same pair of slots, because a screen that fixed the
  teacher and resubmitted would otherwise be told about the second problem
  only on the second attempt. They arrive sorted by scope, then date, then
  weekday, then rule name alphabetically - room, student_set, teacher - which
  is why student_set is first here and not teacher.

  The student_set clash is the interesting one, and it is not obvious. This
  proposal has no subject offering, only a label, and a slot with no offering
  is taken to mean THE WHOLE SECTION: its student set is the section's active
  enrolments rather than an offering's. So supervised study in the same period
  as maths collides with every pupil timetabled into maths. That is what makes
  assembly, games and study hall safe to timetable with a label - they are
  refused against anything the section is already doing.

  THERE IS NO ?force= AND NO OVERRIDE HEADER. A clash cannot be written by any
  caller at any reach. The escape hatch is a deviation on the day, which is a
  different row with a reason on it, rather than a plan that quietly lies
  about where a teacher is.

  No row is written. The three rules are teacher, room and student_set, all on
  by default.
}
